// Source: MCAST Pharma_AP quizzes/Final Quiz.html (Genetics removed)
window.TOPICS = [{"title":"Cell Biology & Tissues","questions":[{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Which organelle is the site of aerobic respiration and produces most of the cell's ATP?","options":["Nucleus","Ribosome","Mitochondrion","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mitochondria are the site of aerobic respiration, where ATP is produced. This is why they are often called the 'powerhouse' of the cell."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Which organelle contains the cell's DNA and controls cell activities, including protein synthesis?","options":["Nucleus","Lysosome","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Cytoskeleton"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The nucleus contains DNA and controls the activities of the cell, including protein synthesis."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"How does rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) differ from smooth endoplasmic reticulum (SER)?","options":["RER contains digestive enzymes","RER has ribosomes on its surface and synthesises and transports proteins","RER produces ATP","RER is only found in plant cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rough ER is 'rough' because ribosomes are attached to its surface. It synthesises and transports proteins. Smooth ER has no ribosomes."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Which organelle synthesises lipids and detoxifies chemicals such as drugs?","options":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Ribosome"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Smooth ER synthesises lipids and detoxifies chemicals (e.g. drugs). It has no ribosomes on its surface."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"What is the function of a ribosome?","options":["To break down waste materials","To read mRNA and assemble amino acids into proteins","To package proteins for secretion","To store the cell's DNA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ribosomes are the site of protein synthesis. They read the mRNA code and join amino acids together in the correct order."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Which organelle modifies and packages proteins for secretion from the cell?","options":["Mitochondrion","Lysosome","Nucleus","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The Golgi apparatus receives proteins (e.g. from the RER), modifies them and packages them into vesicles for secretion."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Lysosomes contain:","options":["DNA","Digestive enzymes that break down waste materials","Ribosomes","Chlorophyll"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lysosomes are membrane-bound sacs of digestive enzymes that break down waste materials and worn-out cell parts."},{"section":"Cell Organelles","q":"Which structure is a network of protein fibres that gives the cell its shape and enables movement?","options":["Cytoskeleton","Plasma membrane","Smooth ER","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cytoskeleton is a network of protein fibres that maintains cell shape and allows movement of the cell and of materials within it."},{"section":"The Plasma Membrane","q":"What is the main type of molecule that forms the plasma membrane?","options":["Cellulose","Phospholipids","Glycogen","Nucleic acids"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The plasma membrane is a phospholipid bilayer — two layers of phospholipid molecules."},{"section":"The Plasma Membrane","q":"How are the phospholipids arranged in the plasma membrane?","options":["Hydrophobic heads face outward; hydrophilic tails face inward","Heads and tails are arranged randomly","Hydrophilic heads face outward towards water; hydrophobic tails face inward","Both heads and tails face the cytoplasm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The hydrophilic ('water-loving') heads face the watery environment inside and outside the cell, while the hydrophobic ('water-fearing') tails face inward, away from water."},{"section":"The Plasma Membrane","q":"The plasma membrane is described as 'selectively permeable'. What does this mean?","options":["All substances can pass through freely","No substances can pass through","Only water can pass through","Only certain substances can pass through, so it controls what enters and leaves the cell"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Selectively permeable means only certain substances can cross the membrane. This allows the cell to control what enters and leaves."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"A tissue is best defined as:","options":["A single specialised cell","A group of similar cells working together to perform a specific function","A group of organs working together","A membrane-bound structure inside a cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tissue is a group of similar cells that work together to carry out a specific function. Tissues combine to form organs."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four main tissue types?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Adipose tissue","Nervous tissue"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four main tissue types are epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous. Adipose tissue is a sub-type of connective tissue, not a main type."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Which tissue type covers body surfaces and lines body cavities?","options":["Epithelial tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue","Connective tissue"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Epithelial tissue covers body surfaces (e.g. skin) and lines cavities and tubes (e.g. the gut and airways)."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Epithelial tissue is classified according to:","options":["Whether it is voluntary or involuntary","The shape of its cells and the number of layers","The type of fibres in its matrix","Its ability to transmit impulses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Epithelia are classified by cell shape (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and number of layers (simple = one layer; stratified = several layers)."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"An epithelium made of a single layer of thin, flat cells — such as the wall of an alveolus — is called:","options":["Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium"],"answer":2,"explanation":"'Simple' means one layer and 'squamous' means flat. Simple squamous epithelium provides a very short diffusion distance, ideal for gas exchange in the alveoli."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Which of the following is an example of connective tissue?","options":["The epidermis of the skin","Blood","Cardiac muscle","A neuron"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connective tissue supports and connects structures. Examples include bone, cartilage, blood and adipose tissue."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Which type of muscle tissue is under voluntary control?","options":["Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","All three types","Skeletal muscle"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Skeletal muscle is voluntary — we choose to contract it. Cardiac muscle (heart) and smooth muscle (internal organs) are involuntary."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Smooth muscle is best described as:","options":["Voluntary muscle attached to bones","Muscle found only in the heart","Involuntary muscle found in the walls of internal organs such as the gut and blood vessels","Tissue that transmits electrical impulses"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Smooth muscle is involuntary and is found in the walls of hollow internal organs, e.g. the gut, bladder and blood vessels."},{"section":"Tissue Types","q":"Nervous tissue is made up of:","options":["Neurons and supporting glial cells","Osteocytes and chondrocytes","Red blood cells and plasma","Adipocytes (fat cells)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Nervous tissue transmits electrical impulses. It is made of neurons (which carry impulses) and glial cells (which support and protect neurons)."}]},{"title":"Gastrointestinal System & Digestion","questions":[{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"Which enzyme begins the digestion of starch in the mouth?","options":["Pepsin","Salivary amylase","Lipase","Trypsin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Salivary amylase in saliva begins the chemical digestion of starch (a polysaccharide) in the mouth."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"What is a bolus?","options":["Partly digested food leaving the stomach","A digestive enzyme","A mass of chewed food mixed with saliva, ready to be swallowed","Undigested waste in the large intestine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A bolus is a mass of chewed food mixed with saliva, formed in the mouth and ready to be swallowed."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"How is the bolus moved down the oesophagus to the stomach?","options":["Peristalsis — waves of muscular contraction","Emulsification","Gravity alone","Diffusion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Peristalsis is the wave-like contraction of the smooth muscle in the oesophagus wall that pushes the bolus towards the stomach."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"Which of the following is a function of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach?","options":["Emulsifying fats","Killing bacteria and denaturing proteins","Digesting starch","Absorbing water"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HCl kills bacteria in food, denatures proteins and provides the acidic conditions needed to activate pepsinogen into pepsin."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"Pepsin is formed in the stomach when HCl activates which inactive precursor?","options":["Bile","Trypsinogen","Maltose","Pepsinogen"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pepsin is secreted in an inactive form, pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin by HCl in the stomach."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"Which organ is the main site of digestion and nutrient absorption?","options":["Stomach","Small intestine","Large intestine","Oesophagus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The small intestine is the main site of chemical digestion and absorption of nutrients into the blood."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"What is the correct order of the three parts of the small intestine, starting from the stomach?","options":["Ileum → jejunum → duodenum","Jejunum → duodenum → ileum","Duodenum → jejunum → ileum","Duodenum → ileum → jejunum"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Food leaves the stomach and passes through the duodenum, then the jejunum, and finally the ileum."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"Which structural feature greatly increases the surface area of the small intestine for absorption?","options":["Villi and microvilli (brush border)","C-shaped cartilage rings","Alveoli","Rugae of the bladder"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Villi are finger-like projections of the intestinal wall, and microvilli (the brush border) cover their cells. Together they massively increase the surface area for absorption."},{"section":"GI Anatomy","q":"What is the main function of the large intestine?","options":["Producing bile","Digesting proteins","Secreting insulin","Absorbing water and electrolytes, and forming and storing faeces"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The large intestine absorbs water and electrolytes from the remaining material and forms and stores faeces."},{"section":"Accessory Organs","q":"Which organ produces bile?","options":["Gallbladder","Pancreas","Liver","Stomach"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bile is produced by the liver. It is then stored and concentrated in the gallbladder."},{"section":"Accessory Organs","q":"What is the role of the gallbladder?","options":["It produces bile","It stores and concentrates bile and releases it into the duodenum","It produces digestive enzymes","It absorbs fats"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The gallbladder stores and concentrates the bile made by the liver and releases it into the duodenum when fatty food arrives."},{"section":"Accessory Organs","q":"What does the pancreas release into the small intestine?","options":["Digestive enzymes (amylase, lipase, proteases) and bicarbonate","Bile salts and HCl","Pepsin and maltase","Saliva"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The pancreas produces pancreatic juice containing amylase, lipase and proteases (e.g. trypsin), plus bicarbonate to neutralise stomach acid."},{"section":"Accessory Organs","q":"Apart from producing bile, which of these is also a function of the liver?","options":["Producing salivary amylase","Absorbing most of the water from food","Churning food","Detoxifying blood and metabolising nutrients"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The liver produces bile, detoxifies the blood (e.g. breaks down drugs and alcohol) and metabolises nutrients."},{"section":"Digestive Enzymes","q":"What is the substrate and product of amylase?","options":["Protein → peptides","Starch → maltose","Lipids → fatty acids and glycerol","Maltose → glucose"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Amylase breaks down starch into maltose. It is found in saliva and pancreatic juice."},{"section":"Digestive Enzymes","q":"Pepsin acts on which type of food molecule?","options":["Starch","Lipids","Maltose","Proteins, breaking them into peptides"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pepsin is a protease that breaks proteins down into peptides in the stomach."},{"section":"Digestive Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme is produced by the pancreas and breaks proteins into smaller peptides?","options":["Pepsin","Trypsin","Maltase","Salivary amylase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trypsin is a protease produced by the pancreas that continues protein digestion in the small intestine."},{"section":"Digestive Enzymes","q":"Where is maltase found, and what does it produce?","options":["In saliva — produces maltose","In the stomach — produces peptides","On the brush border of the small intestine — produces glucose","In bile — produces fatty acids"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Maltase is located on the brush border (microvilli) of the small intestine and breaks down maltose into glucose."},{"section":"Digestive Enzymes","q":"Lipase breaks down lipids into:","options":["Amino acids","Glucose","Fatty acids and glycerol","Maltose"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lipase breaks lipids (fats) down into fatty acids and glycerol. It works together with bile salts."},{"section":"Bile & Lipid Digestion","q":"How do bile salts help in lipid digestion?","options":["They chemically break the bonds in fat molecules","They emulsify lipids — breaking large fat globules into smaller droplets to increase surface area for lipase","They absorb fats directly into the blood","They activate pepsin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bile salts emulsify fats, breaking large globules into smaller droplets. This increases the surface area on which lipase can act."},{"section":"Bile & Lipid Digestion","q":"Emulsification of fats by bile is described as a:","options":["Chemical process catalysed by an enzyme","Physical process — no chemical bonds in the fat are broken","Hormonal process","Type of absorption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Emulsification is physical, not chemical. Bile does not digest fats; lipase is the enzyme that chemically digests them."},{"section":"Mechanical vs Chemical Digestion","q":"Which of the following is an example of MECHANICAL digestion?","options":["Pepsin breaking down protein","Amylase breaking down starch","Chewing food in the mouth","Lipase breaking down fats"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mechanical digestion is the physical breakdown of food into smaller pieces, e.g. chewing (mastication) in the mouth and churning in the stomach."},{"section":"Mechanical vs Chemical Digestion","q":"Chemical digestion is best described as:","options":["The enzymatic breakdown of large molecules into small, soluble molecules","The physical breaking of food into smaller pieces","The movement of food along the gut","The removal of undigested food"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chemical digestion uses enzymes to break large insoluble molecules into small soluble ones that can be absorbed, e.g. pepsin digesting protein."}]},{"title":"Respiratory System","questions":[{"section":"Respiratory Organs","q":"What are the main functions of the nasal cavity?","options":["To filter, warm and humidify inhaled air","To carry out gas exchange","To produce sound","To pump air into the lungs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The nasal cavity filters, warms and humidifies (moistens) air before it reaches the lungs."},{"section":"Respiratory Organs","q":"The nasal cavity and airways are lined with cilia and a mucous membrane. What is their role?","options":["To absorb oxygen","To produce surfactant","Mucus traps dust and microbes, and cilia sweep them away from the lungs","To warm the blood"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mucus traps dust particles and microbes, and the beating cilia move the mucus away from the lungs, protecting the delicate gas exchange surfaces."},{"section":"Respiratory Organs","q":"Why is the trachea reinforced with C-shaped rings of cartilage?","options":["To allow gas exchange","To prevent the trachea from collapsing and keep the airway open","To produce mucus","To contract and control airflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The C-shaped cartilage rings support the trachea so it does not collapse when air pressure inside it falls during inspiration."},{"section":"Respiratory Organs","q":"What is the correct order of the air passages, from the trachea inward?","options":["Trachea → bronchioles → bronchi → alveoli","Trachea → alveoli → bronchi → bronchioles","Bronchi → trachea → bronchioles → alveoli","Trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Air passes down the trachea into the two bronchi, then into the smaller bronchioles, and finally reaches the alveoli."},{"section":"Respiratory Organs","q":"How do bronchioles differ from bronchi?","options":["Bronchioles are larger than bronchi","Bronchioles have thicker cartilage rings","Bronchioles have no cartilage, and smooth muscle in their walls regulates airflow","Bronchioles are the site of gas exchange"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bronchioles are smaller branches with no cartilage. The smooth muscle in their walls can contract or relax to regulate airflow."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"Where does gas exchange take place in the lungs?","options":["Alveoli","Trachea","Bronchi","Nasal cavity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gas exchange occurs in the alveoli — tiny air sacs surrounded by a dense network of capillaries."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"During gas exchange in the alveoli, which way do oxygen and carbon dioxide move?","options":["Both move from the blood into the alveoli","O₂ diffuses from the alveoli into the blood; CO₂ diffuses from the blood into the alveoli","O₂ diffuses from the blood into the alveoli; CO₂ diffuses into the blood","Both are actively pumped into the blood"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each gas diffuses down its concentration gradient: O₂ is higher in the alveoli so it moves into the blood; CO₂ is higher in the blood so it moves into the alveoli to be breathed out."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"Which of the following is NOT an adaptation of the alveoli for gas exchange?","options":["Very thin walls (one cell thick)","A large total surface area","Thick, muscular walls","A rich capillary network"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Alveoli have thin walls, not thick ones. Thin walls give a short diffusion distance. The other features all speed up diffusion."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"Why is it important that the inner surface of the alveoli is moist?","options":["It traps dust","Gases dissolve in the moisture before diffusing across the wall","It makes the alveoli contract","It prevents blood from clotting"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Oxygen and carbon dioxide must dissolve in the moist lining before they can diffuse across the alveolar and capillary walls."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"How is a steep concentration gradient maintained across the alveolar wall?","options":["By thick alveolar walls","By cartilage in the alveoli","By holding the breath","By continuous blood flow through the capillaries and by ventilation (breathing)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Blood flow constantly carries oxygenated blood away and brings CO₂-rich blood, while ventilation brings in fresh air and removes CO₂. This keeps the gradients steep."},{"section":"Gas Exchange","q":"Why do the very thin walls of the alveoli increase the rate of gas exchange?","options":["They provide a short diffusion distance","They increase the concentration of oxygen in the air","They slow down blood flow","They make the alveoli larger"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The thinner the barrier, the shorter the distance gases must travel, so diffusion is faster."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"What happens to the diaphragm during inspiration (breathing in)?","options":["It relaxes and domes upward","It contracts and flattens (moves downward)","It stays still","It contracts and moves upward"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During inspiration the diaphragm contracts and flattens, moving downward. This increases the volume of the thorax."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"What do the external intercostal muscles do during inspiration?","options":["Relax, so the ribs move down and in","Contract, pulling the ribs down","Contract, moving the ribs up and out","They are not involved in breathing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The external intercostal muscles contract, moving the rib cage up and out, which further increases the volume of the thorax."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"During inspiration, what happens to thoracic volume and to the pressure inside the lungs?","options":["Volume increases; pressure falls below atmospheric pressure","Volume decreases; pressure rises above atmospheric pressure","Volume increases; pressure rises","Volume and pressure both stay the same"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Increasing the thoracic volume lowers the pressure inside the lungs below atmospheric pressure, so air flows in."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"Why does air flow INTO the lungs during inspiration?","options":["The lungs actively suck air in","The pressure inside the lungs is lower than atmospheric pressure","The trachea contracts","The alveoli pump air in"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Air always moves from higher to lower pressure. When lung pressure drops below atmospheric pressure, air flows in."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"Which of the following happens during expiration (breathing out) at rest?","options":["The diaphragm contracts and flattens","Thoracic volume increases","Pressure inside the lungs falls","The diaphragm relaxes and domes upward"],"answer":3,"explanation":"During expiration the diaphragm relaxes and domes upward, the rib cage moves down and in, thoracic volume decreases, pressure rises and air flows out."},{"section":"Mechanism of Breathing","q":"What is the diaphragm?","options":["A cartilage ring in the trachea","A membrane lining the alveoli","A dome-shaped sheet of muscle that separates the thorax from the abdomen","A bone of the rib cage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped muscle beneath the lungs. Its contraction and relaxation are the main drivers of breathing."},{"section":"Diffusion (Fick's Law)","q":"Which of the following would INCREASE the rate of diffusion across an exchange surface?","options":["A larger surface area","A thicker membrane","A shallower concentration gradient","Fewer capillaries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"According to Fick's Law, a larger surface area, steeper concentration gradient and thinner membrane all increase the rate of diffusion."},{"section":"Diffusion (Fick's Law)","q":"What effect would a THICKER exchange membrane have on the rate of diffusion?","options":["It would speed up diffusion","It would have no effect","It would reverse the direction of diffusion","It would slow down diffusion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A thicker membrane increases the diffusion distance, so the rate of diffusion decreases."},{"section":"Diffusion (Fick's Law)","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four factors affecting the rate of diffusion (Fick's Law)?","options":["Surface area","Concentration gradient","The number of cilia in the trachea","Thickness of the diffusion membrane"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The four factors are surface area, concentration gradient, thickness of the membrane and the diffusion coefficient. Cilia are not part of Fick's Law."},{"section":"Diffusion (Fick's Law)","q":"The diffusion coefficient in Fick's Law is related to:","options":["The number of alveoli","The size and solubility of the diffusing molecule","The breathing rate","The thickness of the trachea"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The diffusion coefficient depends on properties of the molecule itself, such as its size and solubility."}]},{"title":"Cardiovascular System & Homeostasis","questions":[{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"How many chambers does the human heart have?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The heart has four chambers: the right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium and left ventricle."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Which chamber receives deoxygenated blood from the body via the venae cavae?","options":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Deoxygenated blood returns from the body through the superior and inferior vena cava into the right atrium."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Which chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the body via the aorta?","options":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood out through the aorta to the body's tissues."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"The pulmonary artery carries:","options":["Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium","Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body","Deoxygenated blood from the body to the right atrium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. It is the only artery that carries deoxygenated blood."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"The pulmonary veins carry:","options":["Deoxygenated blood to the lungs","Deoxygenated blood from the body","Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium","Oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium. They are the only veins that carry oxygenated blood."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Which sequence correctly shows the path of blood through the heart?","options":["Vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta","Aorta → left atrium → left ventricle → lungs → right atrium → right ventricle → vena cava","Vena cava → left atrium → left ventricle → lungs → right atrium → right ventricle → aorta","Pulmonary veins → right atrium → right ventricle → aorta → lungs → left atrium → vena cava"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Blood enters the right side from the body, is pumped to the lungs, returns to the left side and is pumped out to the body through the aorta."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Which valve lies between the right atrium and the right ventricle?","options":["Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve","Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium from the right ventricle and prevents backflow of blood into the atrium."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Which valve lies between the left atrium and the left ventricle?","options":["Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve","Mitral (bicuspid) valve","Aortic valve"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The mitral (bicuspid) valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"What is the name of the wall that separates the left and right ventricles?","options":["Atrial septum","Pericardium","Mitral valve","Ventricular septum"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The ventricular septum separates the two ventricles, preventing oxygenated and deoxygenated blood from mixing."},{"section":"Heart Structure","q":"Why is the wall of the left ventricle thicker than that of the right ventricle?","options":["It holds a larger volume of blood","It must pump blood at higher pressure all around the body","It receives blood from the lungs","It contains more valves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The left ventricle pumps blood around the whole body, which requires much more force than pumping blood the short distance to the lungs."},{"section":"Cardiac Output","q":"How is cardiac output calculated?","options":["Heart rate × stroke volume","Heart rate ÷ stroke volume","Stroke volume − heart rate","Blood pressure × heart rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. It is the volume of blood pumped by the heart per minute."},{"section":"Cardiac Output","q":"What is stroke volume?","options":["The number of heartbeats per minute","The total blood volume of the body","The volume of blood pumped by a ventricle per beat","The pressure in the aorta"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out of a ventricle with each heartbeat."},{"section":"Cardiac Output","q":"A person has a heart rate of 70 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 70 mL. What is their cardiac output?","options":["140 mL/min","4900 mL/min (4.9 L/min)","1 mL/min","70 L/min"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cardiac output = 70 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 4900 mL/min, which is about 4.9 L per minute — a typical resting value."},{"section":"Cardiovascular Homeostasis","q":"During exercise, which change in the blood is detected by chemoreceptors?","options":["A fall in CO₂ and a rise in pH","A rise in glucose","A fall in temperature","A rise in CO₂ and a fall in pH"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Working muscles produce more CO₂, which lowers blood pH. Chemoreceptors detect the rise in CO₂ and fall in pH."},{"section":"Cardiovascular Homeostasis","q":"In response to the signals from chemoreceptors during exercise, what happens to heart rate and stroke volume?","options":["Both increase, raising cardiac output","Both decrease, lowering cardiac output","Heart rate increases but stroke volume falls to zero","Neither changes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Heart rate and stroke volume both increase, so cardiac output rises and more blood reaches the muscles."},{"section":"Cardiovascular Homeostasis","q":"How does the increase in cardiac output during exercise help maintain O₂/CO₂ homeostasis?","options":["It stores oxygen in the heart","It stops the muscles from producing CO₂","O₂ is delivered faster to the muscles and CO₂ is removed more efficiently","It lowers body temperature directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A higher cardiac output delivers oxygen to the working muscles faster and carries CO₂ away to the lungs more efficiently, keeping blood gas levels stable."},{"section":"Cardiovascular Homeostasis","q":"During exercise body temperature rises. How do blood vessels in the skin respond?","options":["They vasoconstrict to keep heat in","They vasodilate, bringing more blood to the skin surface so heat is lost","They close completely","They carry less blood to the skin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vasodilation (widening) of skin blood vessels increases blood flow near the surface, allowing heat to be lost to the environment."},{"section":"Cardiovascular Homeostasis","q":"In a cold environment, how do blood vessels in the skin help conserve heat?","options":["They vasodilate","They increase blood flow to the skin","They leak plasma","They vasoconstrict, reducing blood flow to the skin surface"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vasoconstriction (narrowing) reduces blood flow to the periphery, so less heat is lost through the skin."},{"section":"Disease & Lifestyle","q":"What is atherosclerosis?","options":["A build-up of fatty plaques in artery walls that narrows the arteries","An infection of the heart valves","A hole in the ventricular septum","An abnormally slow heart rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Atherosclerosis is the build-up of fatty plaques in the walls of arteries. It narrows the arteries and reduces blood flow."},{"section":"Disease & Lifestyle","q":"How does coronary artery disease disrupt homeostasis?","options":["It increases oxygen supply to the heart","It causes the lungs to stop working","Narrowed coronary arteries reduce O₂ supply to heart muscle, lowering cardiac output and impairing O₂/CO₂ homeostasis","It only affects the veins"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Atherosclerosis narrows the coronary arteries → less O₂ to cardiac muscle → reduced cardiac output → less efficient O₂ delivery and CO₂ removal throughout the body."},{"section":"Disease & Lifestyle","q":"Which of the following lifestyle factors INCREASES the risk of cardiovascular disease?","options":["Regular aerobic exercise","Smoking","A diet rich in fruit and vegetables","Adequate sleep"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Smoking damages artery walls, promotes atherosclerosis and raises blood pressure, all of which impair cardiovascular homeostasis."},{"section":"Disease & Lifestyle","q":"How does a diet high in saturated fat mainly impair cardiovascular homeostasis?","options":["It lowers blood pressure","It strengthens the heart muscle","It reduces heart rate","It raises blood cholesterol, promoting fatty plaques (atherosclerosis) in the arteries"],"answer":3,"explanation":"High saturated fat intake raises blood cholesterol, which contributes to plaque formation, narrowing arteries and reducing blood flow."}]},{"title":"Lymphatic & Immune System","questions":[{"section":"The Lymphatic System","q":"What is lymph?","options":["Blood plasma inside the arteries","Fluid derived from interstitial (tissue) fluid that drains into lymphatic vessels","A type of white blood cell","Fluid produced by the kidneys"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lymph is formed from interstitial fluid (the fluid bathing the cells) that drains into lymphatic vessels."},{"section":"The Lymphatic System","q":"What is the main function of lymphatic vessels?","options":["To return lymph (excess tissue fluid) to the blood","To carry oxygen to the tissues","To pump blood around the body","To produce red blood cells"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Lymphatic vessels are one-way vessels that collect excess tissue fluid and return it to the bloodstream."},{"section":"The Lymphatic System","q":"Which of the following describes lymphatic vessels compared with blood vessels?","options":["They form a complete loop with the heart","They have thicker walls than arteries","They are closed at one end, have thinner walls and contain valves","They contain red blood cells"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lymphatic vessels begin as blind-ended (closed) tubes, have thinner walls than blood vessels and contain valves."},{"section":"The Lymphatic System","q":"What is the function of the valves in lymphatic vessels?","options":["To filter bacteria","To produce antibodies","To increase pressure","To prevent the backflow of lymph"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Valves keep lymph flowing in one direction — towards the blood — and prevent backflow."},{"section":"The Lymphatic System","q":"Which of the following is NOT normally found in lymph?","options":["Water","Red blood cells","Lymphocytes (white blood cells)","Dissolved substances such as salts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lymph does not contain red blood cells. It contains water, dissolved substances and white blood cells such as lymphocytes."},{"section":"Antigens & Antibodies","q":"What is an antigen?","options":["A foreign molecule (usually a protein) that triggers an immune response","A Y-shaped protein made by plasma cells","A type of white blood cell","A hormone that activates B cells"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An antigen is a foreign molecule, usually a protein (e.g. on the surface of a pathogen), that triggers an immune response."},{"section":"Antigens & Antibodies","q":"What is an epitope?","options":["A type of lymphocyte","A lymphatic vessel","The specific binding site on an antigen recognised by an antibody or receptor","The stem of the antibody molecule"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An epitope is the specific region of an antigen that an antibody (or T-cell receptor) recognises and binds to."},{"section":"Antigens & Antibodies","q":"Which of the following correctly describes an antibody?","options":["A foreign protein on a pathogen","A type of red blood cell","A fluid in lymphatic vessels","A Y-shaped protein (immunoglobulin) that binds specifically to its antigen"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins called immunoglobulins. Each binds specifically to one antigen."},{"section":"Antigens & Antibodies","q":"Which cells secrete large quantities of antibodies?","options":["T helper cells","Plasma cells","Red blood cells","Memory B cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plasma cells are differentiated B cells that secrete large quantities of antibodies."},{"section":"Antigens & Antibodies","q":"Why does each antibody bind to only one particular antigen?","options":["Its binding site has a shape complementary to one specific epitope","All antibodies are identical","Antibodies bind to any foreign molecule","Antigens change shape to fit the antibody"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The binding site of each antibody has a specific shape that is complementary to one epitope, so it binds only to that antigen."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"B cells are responsible for which type of immunity?","options":["Cell-mediated immunity only","Innate (non-specific) immunity","Humoral immunity","Passive immunity only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"B cells are responsible for humoral immunity — antibody-mediated immunity."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"What is humoral immunity?","options":["Immunity provided by the skin","Immunity involving phagocytes only","Immunity provided by hormones","Antibody-mediated immunity involving B cells and antibodies"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Humoral immunity is antibody-mediated immunity. It involves B cells and the antibodies they produce."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"How does a B cell first recognise a specific antigen?","options":["By engulfing all foreign particles","Through a specific receptor on its surface that binds the antigen","By detecting a change in temperature","Through signals from red blood cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each B cell has receptors on its surface specific to one antigen. The B cell is selected when its receptor binds that antigen."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"Which cell helps to activate B cells during the adaptive immune response?","options":["T helper cell","Red blood cell","Platelet","Plasma cell"],"answer":0,"explanation":"T helper cells activate B cells (and cytotoxic T cells), orchestrating the adaptive immune response."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"After activation, B cells proliferate and differentiate into which TWO types of cell?","options":["Red blood cells and platelets","T helper cells and cytotoxic T cells","Plasma cells and memory B cells","Macrophages and neutrophils"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activated B cells divide and differentiate into plasma cells (which secrete antibodies) and memory B cells (which give long-term protection)."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"Which sequence correctly describes the B cell response to a foreign antigen?","options":["Antibodies released → B cell recognises antigen → antigen enters body → plasma cells form","Memory cells form → antigen enters → T helper cells destroy the antigen directly","Plasma cells recognise antigen → B cells secrete antibodies → T helper cells form","Antigen enters → B cell receptor recognises antigen → B cell activated (with T helper cell) → plasma cells secrete antibodies → antibodies neutralise antigen"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Antigen enters → B cell recognises it → activation with T helper cell help → proliferation and differentiation into plasma cells and memory B cells → antibodies neutralise the antigen."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"What is the role of memory B cells?","options":["To produce antibodies during the first infection","To remain in the body long-term and allow a rapid response on re-exposure to the same antigen","To phagocytose pathogens","To carry lymph back to the blood"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Memory B cells are long-lived. If the same antigen enters again, they rapidly produce plasma cells, giving a faster and stronger secondary response."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"Compared with the primary immune response, the secondary immune response is:","options":["Faster and stronger, producing more antibodies","Slower and weaker","Exactly the same","Only non-specific"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because memory B cells are already present, the secondary response is faster, stronger and produces more antibodies — often before symptoms develop."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"What is the main role of T helper cells?","options":["To secrete antibodies","To carry oxygen","To activate B cells and cytotoxic T cells, coordinating the adaptive immune response","To form lymph"],"answer":2,"explanation":"T helper cells orchestrate the adaptive immune response by activating B cells and cytotoxic T cells."},{"section":"B Cells & Humoral Immunity","q":"How do antibodies help to protect the body once they bind to an antigen?","options":["They carry the antigen to the kidneys for excretion","They convert the antigen into glucose","They turn into memory cells","They neutralise the antigen, e.g. blocking toxins or pathogens and marking them for destruction"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Antibodies bind to antigens and neutralise them — blocking the pathogen or toxin and marking it for destruction by other immune cells."}]},{"title":"Osmoregulation & Excretion","questions":[{"section":"The Urinary System","q":"What is the main role of the urinary system?","options":["To digest food and absorb nutrients","To remove waste products and excess substances from the blood","To transport oxygen to body cells","To produce hormones for growth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The urinary system removes waste products and excess substances from the blood, maintaining fluid and chemical balance. It is essential for homeostasis."},{"section":"The Urinary System","q":"Which four organs make up the urinary system?","options":["Heart, lungs, liver, kidneys","Kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra","Kidneys, stomach, bladder, bowel","Liver, kidneys, spleen, bladder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The four main organs of the urinary system are the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra."},{"section":"The Urinary System","q":"Which of the following is NOT a key function of the kidneys?","options":["Filtration of blood","Excretion of urea","Production of red blood cells directly","Regulation of water and salts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kidneys filter blood, excrete urea, and regulate water and salts. They produce hormones like erythropoietin but do not produce red blood cells directly."},{"section":"Excretion, Egestion & Secretion","q":"What is excretion?","options":["The removal of undigested food from the body","The elimination of waste metabolic substances from the body","The process of producing and releasing a useful substance","The absorption of nutrients into the blood"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Excretion is the elimination of waste metabolic substances from the body which, if permitted to accumulate, would prevent the maintenance of a steady state."},{"section":"Excretion, Egestion & Secretion","q":"What is egestion?","options":["The removal of metabolic waste from cells","The elimination of undigested food that was never involved in cell metabolism","The release of useful hormones into the blood","The reabsorption of water in the kidney"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Egestion is the elimination of waste substances, mainly undigested food, which have never been involved in the metabolic activities of cells."},{"section":"Excretion, Egestion & Secretion","q":"Osmoregulation refers to:","options":["The removal of carbon dioxide from the lungs","The maintenance of constant osmotic conditions in the body","The breakdown of proteins into amino acids","The filtration of blood in the glomerulus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Osmoregulation is the maintenance of constant osmotic conditions in the body, involving the control of the gain and loss of water and solutes."},{"section":"Nitrogenous Waste & Urea","q":"Nitrogenous waste products are produced by the breakdown of which substances?","options":["Carbohydrates and fats","Proteins, nucleic acids, and excess amino acids","Glucose and glycogen","Vitamins and minerals"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nitrogenous waste products are produced by the breakdown of proteins, nucleic acids, and excess amino acids."},{"section":"Nitrogenous Waste & Urea","q":"Which of the following correctly describes ammonia?","options":["Low toxicity and easily stored in the body","Highly toxic and cannot be stored in the body","Insoluble in water","Requires large amounts of energy to form"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ammonia is highly toxic, extremely soluble, highly diffusible through water, and cannot be stored in the body. No energy is needed in its formation."},{"section":"Nitrogenous Waste & Urea","q":"Ammonia may be converted into which two substances before excretion?","options":["Glucose and glycogen","Carbon dioxide and water","Urea or uric acid","Creatinine and bile"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ammonia may be excreted immediately or converted into urea or uric acid before excretion."},{"section":"Nitrogenous Waste & Urea","q":"What is the principal nitrogenous waste product excreted by mammals?","options":["Ammonia","Uric acid","Urea","Creatinine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mammals excrete urea as their principal nitrogenous waste product. The organ responsible for excreting urea is the kidney."},{"section":"The Kidney","q":"Where in the body are the kidneys located?","options":["In the chest cavity, behind the lungs","In the lower back","In the abdomen, next to the stomach","In the pelvis, beside the bladder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The kidneys are bean-shaped organs located in the lower back, divided into an outer cortex and an inner medulla."},{"section":"The Kidney","q":"What is the name of the outer region of the kidney?","options":["Medulla","Pelvis","Cortex","Capsule"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kidney is divided into two main regions: the cortex (outer) and the medulla (inner)."},{"section":"The Kidney","q":"The kidneys are described as bean-shaped organs. What surrounds them for protection?","options":["A layer of fat only","A tough capsule","The peritoneum only","The rib cage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each kidney is surrounded by a tough capsule that provides protection."},{"section":"The Kidney","q":"Which of the following is a hormone produced by the kidneys?","options":["Insulin","Erythropoietin","ADH","Aldosterone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The kidneys produce hormones including erythropoietin (which stimulates red blood cell production) and renin."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the name of the functional unit of the kidney?","options":["Alveolus","Nephron","Glomerulus","Villus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The nephron is the functional subunit of the kidney. Each kidney contains about 1 million nephrons."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the glomerulus?","options":["A tube that carries urine to the bladder","A knot of capillaries that is the site of blood filtration","A cup-shaped structure that collects filtrate","A duct that collects urine from multiple nephrons"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The glomerulus is a knot of capillaries inside the nephron and is the site of blood filtration."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is Bowman's capsule?","options":["The tube that connects the kidney to the bladder","A cup-shaped structure that receives the glomerular filtrate","The outer protective covering of the kidney","The duct that adjusts water reabsorption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bowman's capsule is the cup-shaped structure that surrounds the glomerulus and receives the glomerular filtrate."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"During glomerular filtration, what passes from the glomerulus into Bowman's capsule?","options":["Only red blood cells","Large proteins and blood cells","Water and small molecules","Only urea"],"answer":2,"explanation":"During glomerular filtration, blood pressure forces water and small molecules (such as glucose, ions, and urea) from the glomerulus into Bowman's capsule."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What happens during tubular reabsorption?","options":["Waste products are added to the filtrate","Useful substances such as glucose, amino acids, and ions are reabsorbed back into the blood","Water is removed from the blood into the tubule","Urine is transported to the bladder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During tubular reabsorption, useful substances including glucose, amino acids, and ions are reabsorbed from the filtrate back into the blood, mainly in the proximal tubule."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is tubular secretion?","options":["The reabsorption of glucose from the filtrate","The filtration of blood in the glomerulus","Additional wastes and excess ions are actively moved from the blood into the tubule","The production of ADH in the hypothalamus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tubular secretion is the process where additional wastes and excess ions (like H⁺ and K⁺) are actively transported from the blood into the tubule for excretion."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"ADH increases the permeability to water of which two structures in the nephron?","options":["Glomerulus and Bowman's capsule","Loop of Henle and glomerulus","Collecting duct and distal tubule cells","Proximal tubule and Bowman's capsule"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ADH increases the permeability of both the collecting duct cells and the distal tubule cells to water, promoting water reabsorption."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the order in which filtrate flows through the nephron?","options":["PCT → Bowman's capsule → DCT → loop of Henle → collecting duct","Bowman's capsule → PCT → loop of Henle → DCT → collecting duct","Bowman's capsule → loop of Henle → PCT → collecting duct → DCT","Collecting duct → DCT → loop of Henle → PCT → Bowman's capsule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filtrate passes from Bowman's capsule into the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), round the loop of Henle, into the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and then into the collecting duct."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the main role of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)?","options":["Filtering blood under high pressure","Creating a concentration gradient in the medulla","Reabsorbing glucose, amino acids, water and salts back into the blood","Storing urine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The PCT is the main site of reabsorption: glucose, amino acids, water and salts are returned to the blood."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"In a healthy person, how much of the filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the PCT?","options":["None","About half","A small amount","All of it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ALL glucose and amino acids are normally reabsorbed in the PCT, along with most of the mineral salts and most of the water. This is why glucose is not normally found in urine."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"By which process is water reabsorbed from the nephron?","options":["Osmosis","Active transport","Filtration","Exocytosis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Water is reabsorbed by osmosis — in the PCT, the loop of Henle and (under the control of ADH) the collecting duct."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the main function of the loop of Henle?","options":["Filtering blood","Reabsorbing all the glucose","Creating a concentration gradient in the renal medulla, which is important for water reabsorption","Producing ADH"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loop of Henle creates a concentration (salt) gradient in the renal medulla. This allows water to be reabsorbed by osmosis, producing concentrated urine."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the role of the distal convoluted tubule (DCT)?","options":["It is the site of glomerular filtration","It fine-tunes reabsorption under the control of hormones such as ADH and aldosterone","It collects urine from many nephrons","It carries urine to the bladder"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The DCT fine-tunes the reabsorption of salts and water, and is regulated by hormones such as ADH and aldosterone."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What is the function of the collecting duct?","options":["It filters blood under pressure","It reabsorbs all the glucose","It produces renin","It collects filtrate from many nephrons, and ADH regulates how much water it reabsorbs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The collecting duct receives filtrate from several nephrons. ADH makes it more permeable to water, so more water is reabsorbed when the body needs it."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"Why are proteins and red blood cells NOT normally found in the glomerular filtrate?","options":["They are too large to pass through the filtration membrane","They are reabsorbed in the PCT","They are destroyed in Bowman's capsule","They are removed by the loop of Henle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Proteins and blood cells are too large to pass through the glomerular filtration membrane, so they stay in the blood. Only small molecules (water, glucose, urea, ions) are filtered."},{"section":"The Nephron","q":"What does the urine leaving the collecting duct contain?","options":["Glucose, amino acids and proteins","Red blood cells and plasma proteins","Metabolic waste (urea, creatinine, uric acid) plus excess water and mineral salts","Only water"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Urine contains metabolic wastes such as urea, creatinine and uric acid, together with any excess water and mineral salts."},{"section":"Hormonal Control","q":"What does ADH stand for?","options":["Aldosterone Dehydration Hormone","Antidiuretic Hormone","Adrenal Dehydrating Hormone","Anterior Diuresis Hormone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ADH stands for Antidiuretic Hormone. It acts against excessive urine production by increasing water reabsorption."},{"section":"Hormonal Control","q":"What is the effect of ADH on the kidney?","options":["It decreases water reabsorption, producing more dilute urine","It increases water reabsorption, producing more concentrated urine","It increases glucose reabsorption","It stops filtration in the glomerulus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ADH increases the permeability of the collecting duct and distal tubule cells to water, increasing water reabsorption and producing more concentrated urine."},{"section":"Hormonal Control","q":"What does aldosterone promote in the kidney?","options":["Glucose reabsorption","Urea secretion","Sodium reabsorption","Water filtration in the glomerulus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Aldosterone promotes sodium reabsorption in the kidney tubules, which in turn influences water retention and blood pressure."},{"section":"Hormonal Control","q":"Where is ADH produced?","options":["The posterior pituitary gland","The kidneys","The hypothalamus","The adrenal cortex"],"answer":2,"explanation":"ADH is produced by neurons in the hypothalamus and released from nerve endings in the posterior pituitary gland into the bloodstream."}]},{"title":"Endocrine System","questions":[{"section":"Glands & Hormones","q":"What is an endocrine gland?","options":["A gland that secretes enzymes through a duct","A ductless gland that secretes hormones directly into the blood","A gland that produces sweat","A gland found only in the brain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Endocrine glands have no ducts. They secrete hormones directly into the bloodstream."},{"section":"Glands & Hormones","q":"How does an exocrine gland differ from an endocrine gland?","options":["It secretes hormones into the blood","It has no duct","It is found only in the pancreas","It has a duct and secretes substances (e.g. sweat, enzymes) onto a surface or into a cavity"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Exocrine glands release their secretions through a duct onto a surface or into a cavity, e.g. sweat glands and salivary glands."},{"section":"Glands & Hormones","q":"Which is the best definition of a hormone?","options":["A chemical messenger produced by an endocrine gland, carried in the blood to target cells with specific receptors","An electrical impulse travelling along a neuron","A digestive enzyme","A protein that binds to antigens"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A hormone is a chemical messenger secreted by an endocrine gland, transported in the blood, which acts on target cells that have specific receptors."},{"section":"Glands & Hormones","q":"Why does a hormone only affect certain cells even though it travels all around the body in the blood?","options":["It is destroyed before it reaches most cells","Only some cells have blood vessels","Only target cells have the specific receptors for that hormone","Hormones only travel to the brain"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only target cells possess the specific receptors that the hormone can bind to, so only those cells respond."},{"section":"Coordination Centres","q":"What are the TWO major coordination centres of the endocrine system?","options":["Thyroid and adrenal glands","Hypothalamus and pituitary gland","Pancreas and liver","Ovaries and testes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The hypothalamus and pituitary gland work together to coordinate and control the endocrine system."},{"section":"Coordination Centres","q":"Which brain structure links the nervous and endocrine systems and controls the pituitary gland?","options":["Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Thyroid gland","Medulla"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The hypothalamus coordinates the nervous and endocrine systems and controls the release of hormones from the pituitary gland."},{"section":"Coordination Centres","q":"Why is the pituitary gland often called the 'master gland'?","options":["It is the largest gland in the body","It produces insulin","It controls heart rate directly","It releases hormones (e.g. TSH, FSH, LH) that control other endocrine glands"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The pituitary releases hormones such as TSH, FSH and LH that stimulate other glands (e.g. thyroid, ovaries, testes) to secrete their own hormones."},{"section":"Endocrine Glands","q":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is released by the pituitary gland. Which gland does it act on?","options":["Adrenal gland","Pancreas","Thyroid gland","Ovary"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroxine."},{"section":"Endocrine Glands","q":"Thyroxine (T3/T4), produced by the thyroid gland, mainly regulates:","options":["Blood glucose","Metabolic rate","Water reabsorption","Sperm production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thyroxine regulates the body's metabolic rate — how quickly cells use energy."},{"section":"Endocrine Glands","q":"Which TWO hormones are produced by the adrenal glands?","options":["Adrenaline and cortisol","Insulin and glucagon","Oestrogen and progesterone","TSH and ADH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The adrenal glands produce adrenaline (fight or flight) and cortisol (the stress hormone)."},{"section":"Endocrine Glands","q":"Which hormone prepares the body for 'fight or flight' by raising heart rate and dilating the pupils?","options":["Insulin","Thyroxine","Glucagon","Adrenaline"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adrenaline, from the adrenal glands, prepares the body for fight or flight — increasing heart rate, dilating pupils and raising blood glucose."},{"section":"Endocrine Glands","q":"Why is the pancreas described as both an endocrine AND an exocrine gland?","options":["It produces bile and saliva","It is found in two places in the body","It secretes hormones (insulin, glucagon) into the blood and digestive enzymes through a duct","It controls the pituitary gland"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The pancreas secretes insulin and glucagon directly into the blood (endocrine) and digestive enzymes via the pancreatic duct into the small intestine (exocrine)."},{"section":"Blood Glucose Homeostasis","q":"When blood glucose rises, which cells detect the rise and secrete insulin?","options":["Alpha cells of the pancreas","Beta cells of the pancreas","Liver cells","Adrenal cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Beta cells in the pancreas detect the rise in blood glucose and secrete insulin."},{"section":"Blood Glucose Homeostasis","q":"What is the effect of insulin?","options":["It lowers blood glucose by stimulating liver and muscle cells to take up glucose and store it as glycogen","It raises blood glucose by breaking down glycogen","It increases metabolic rate","It increases water reabsorption in the kidney"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Insulin stimulates liver and muscle cells to take up glucose and store it as glycogen, so blood glucose falls."},{"section":"Blood Glucose Homeostasis","q":"Which hormone is secreted when blood glucose FALLS, and by which cells?","options":["Insulin, by beta cells","Adrenaline, by alpha cells","Thyroxine, by the thyroid","Glucagon, by alpha cells of the pancreas"],"answer":3,"explanation":"When blood glucose falls, alpha cells in the pancreas secrete glucagon."},{"section":"Blood Glucose Homeostasis","q":"What does glucagon stimulate the liver to do?","options":["Store glucose as glycogen","Produce bile","Convert glycogen back into glucose, raising blood glucose","Produce insulin"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Glucagon stimulates the liver to break down glycogen into glucose, which is released into the blood so blood glucose rises."},{"section":"Blood Glucose Homeostasis","q":"What is glycogen?","options":["A hormone that lowers blood glucose","The storage form of glucose in the liver and muscles","An enzyme that digests starch","A type of fat"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Glycogen is a large carbohydrate made from many glucose molecules. It is the storage form of glucose in the liver and muscles."},{"section":"Negative Feedback","q":"What is negative feedback?","options":["A change from the normal level is detected and a response reverses it, returning the level towards normal","A change is detected and the response makes the change even bigger","A process that only occurs in the nervous system","A hormone that stops working"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In negative feedback, a change is detected and the response opposes (reverses) it, bringing the level back to its set point."},{"section":"Negative Feedback","q":"After a carbohydrate-rich meal, which sequence is correct?","options":["Blood glucose falls → glucagon released → glycogen stored","Blood glucose rises → alpha cells secrete glucagon → blood glucose rises further","Blood glucose rises → adrenaline released → glucose stored in the kidney","Blood glucose rises → beta cells secrete insulin → cells take up glucose → blood glucose falls back to normal"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Rising glucose → beta cells secrete insulin → liver and muscle take up glucose and store it as glycogen → blood glucose returns to normal. This is negative feedback."},{"section":"Negative Feedback","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which hormone levels are regulated in the body?","options":["Positive feedback","Voluntary control","Negative feedback","Reflex arcs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Most hormone levels are controlled by negative feedback: a rising hormone level (or its effect) feeds back to reduce further secretion."}]},{"title":"Sensory Organ System","questions":[{"section":"The Eye","q":"How many special senses are there in total?","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"answer":2,"explanation":"There are five special senses: vision, hearing, taste, smell, and touch."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is the transparent 'window' at the front of the eye that light first passes through?","options":["Sclera","Iris","Cornea","Retina"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cornea is the transparent central anterior portion of the fibrous layer through which light enters the eye."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is the white of the eye called?","options":["Cornea","Choroid","Sclera","Iris"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sclera is the thick, glistening, white connective tissue seen anteriorly as the 'white of the eye'."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is the name of the coloured, ring-shaped structure that controls how much light enters the eye?","options":["Pupil","Iris","Ciliary body","Choroid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The iris is the pigmented structure with a rounded opening called the pupil, through which light passes into the eye."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is the name of the opening in the iris through which light passes?","options":["Fovea","Optic disc","Pupil","Canal of Schlemm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The pupil is the rounded opening in the pigmented iris through which light passes."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"The receptor cells in the retina that respond to light are called:","options":["Bipolar cells","Ganglion cells","Photoreceptors","Gustatory cells"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The rods and cones in the neural layer of the retina are called photoreceptors because they respond to light."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"The optic disc is also known as the:","options":["Fovea centralis","Blind spot","Vitreous body","Ciliary body"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The optic disc is the site where the optic nerve leaves the eyeball. Because there are no photoreceptors here, it is called the blind spot."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"The watery fluid found in the front segment of the eye is called:","options":["Vitreous humor","Perilymph","Aqueous humor","Endolymph"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The anterior (aqueous) segment of the eye contains a clear, watery fluid called aqueous humor."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Which structure produces an oily secretion that lubricates the eye?","options":["Lacrimal gland","Conjunctiva","Tarsal glands","Ciliary glands"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The tarsal glands are modified sebaceous glands at the eyelid edges that produce an oily secretion to lubricate the eye."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Lacrimal secretions (tears) contain an enzyme called lysozyme. What does it do?","options":["Focuses light on the retina","Destroys bacteria on the eye surface","Produces aqueous humor","Controls the size of the pupil"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lysozyme is an enzyme found in tears that destroys bacteria, cleansing and protecting the eye surface."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Where does visual interpretation finally take place in the brain?","options":["Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The optic radiation runs to the occipital lobe of the brain where visual interpretation (seeing) occurs."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is the name of the delicate membrane that lines the eyelids and covers part of the outer surface of the eyeball?","options":["Cornea","Conjunctiva","Sclera","Choroid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The conjunctiva is a delicate membrane that lines the eyelids and covers part of the outer surface of the eyeball, ending at the edge of the cornea."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Both the cornea and the lens refract (bend) light. What is the key difference between them?","options":["The cornea has fixed refraction; the lens is adjustable via the ciliary muscles","The lens has fixed refraction; the cornea is adjustable","Only the lens refracts light","The cornea detects colour; the lens detects movement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cornea refracts light by a fixed amount. The lens can change shape (accommodation) using the ciliary muscles, so its refraction is adjustable for fine focusing."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"What is meant by accommodation of the eye?","options":["Adjusting pupil size in bright light","Changing the shape of the lens to focus light on the retina at different distances","Producing tears to protect the cornea","Converting light into nerve impulses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accommodation is the change in the shape (thickness) of the lens, controlled by the ciliary muscles, so that light from near or distant objects is focused on the retina."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Which muscles change the shape of the lens?","options":["The iris muscles","The extrinsic eye muscles","The ciliary muscles","The orbicularis oculi"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ciliary muscles change the shape of the lens during accommodation."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"When looking at a DISTANT object, what happens to the ciliary muscles and the lens?","options":["Ciliary muscles contract; lens becomes more rounded","Ciliary muscles contract; lens flattens","Ciliary muscles relax; lens becomes more rounded","Ciliary muscles relax; lens flattens (becomes thinner)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"For distant objects the ciliary muscles relax and the lens flattens, so light is bent less."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"When looking at a NEAR object, what happens to the ciliary muscles and the lens?","options":["Ciliary muscles contract; lens becomes more rounded (thicker)","Ciliary muscles relax; lens flattens","Ciliary muscles relax; lens becomes more rounded","The lens does not change shape"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For near objects the ciliary muscles contract and the lens becomes more rounded, bending light more strongly to focus it on the retina."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Which statement correctly describes rods in the retina?","options":["They require bright light and detect colour","They are sensitive to low light levels and do not detect colour","They are concentrated in the fovea","They refract light onto the lens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rods work in low light and detect movement, but do not detect colour — they give black-and-white vision."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Which statement correctly describes cones in the retina?","options":["They work best in dim light","They give black-and-white vision only","They require bright light and detect colour","They are found only at the blind spot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cones require bright light and are responsible for colour vision and detailed vision."},{"section":"The Eye","q":"Which photoreceptors are concentrated in the fovea, the area of sharpest vision?","options":["Rods","Ganglion cells","Bipolar cells","Cones"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cones are concentrated in the fovea, which is why this area gives the most detailed, colour vision."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is the shell-shaped outer part of the ear, visible on the side of the head, called?","options":["External acoustic meatus","Tympanic membrane","Auricle (pinna)","Ossicle"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The auricle, or pinna, is the shell-shaped structure surrounding the opening of the auditory canal."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is another name for the eardrum?","options":["Oval window","Tympanic membrane","Round window","Basilar membrane"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The tympanic membrane is commonly known as the eardrum. Sound waves cause it to vibrate."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"The three smallest bones in the body are found in the middle ear. What are they collectively called?","options":["Ossicles","Labyrinth","Semicircular canals","Vestibules"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The three tiny bones in the middle ear are called ossicles. They are the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil), and stapes (stirrup)."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is the Eustachian (pharyngotympanic) tube?","options":["A tube connecting the inner ear to the cochlea","A tube linking the middle ear to the throat","A canal draining fluid from the bony labyrinth","A channel connecting the outer ear to the eardrum"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pharyngotympanic (Eustachian) tube links the middle ear cavity with the throat, allowing pressure equalisation."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"Where is the cochlea located?","options":["In the outer ear","In the middle ear","In the inner ear","Behind the eye socket"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cochlea is one of the three subdivisions of the bony labyrinth, located in the inner ear deep within the temporal bone."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is the main function of the cochlea?","options":["To maintain balance and posture","To equalise pressure in the ear","To convert sound vibrations into nerve impulses for hearing","To produce earwax"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cochlea is the organ of hearing. Vibrations cause the hair cells in the organ of Corti to bend, generating electrical signals that travel to the brain as nerve impulses."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is the correct order of the three ossicles, from the eardrum inward?","options":["Incus → Malleus → Stapes","Stapes → Incus → Malleus","Malleus → Incus → Stapes","Malleus → Stapes → Incus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vibrations travel from the eardrum to the malleus, then to the incus, and then to the stapes, which moves the oval window."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"What is the name of the nerve that carries sound impulses from the ear to the brain?","options":["Optic nerve","Olfactory nerve","Vestibulocochlear nerve","Facial nerve"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nerve impulses from the cochlear nerve are conveyed to the auditory area of the brain via the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"The stapes vibrates against which structure, setting up fluid waves in the cochlea?","options":["The tympanic membrane","The oval window","The round window","The auricle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The stapes pushes on the oval window, which creates pressure waves in the fluid of the cochlea."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"Where are the hair cells (mechanoreceptors) that convert vibrations into nerve impulses found?","options":["In the organ of Corti inside the cochlea","In the tympanic membrane","In the Eustachian tube","In the auricle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The hair cells of the organ of Corti in the cochlea are mechanoreceptors: fluid waves bend them, generating nerve impulses in the auditory nerve."},{"section":"The Ear","q":"Which sequence correctly traces sound from the outer ear to the brain?","options":["Tympanic membrane → cochlea → ossicles → auditory canal → brain","Auditory canal → oval window → tympanic membrane → ossicles → brain","Auditory canal → tympanic membrane → malleus → incus → stapes → oval window → cochlea (organ of Corti) → auditory nerve → brain","Cochlea → stapes → incus → malleus → tympanic membrane → brain"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sound waves travel down the auditory canal, vibrate the eardrum, pass through the malleus, incus and stapes to the oval window, create fluid waves in the cochlea, and hair cells send impulses along the auditory nerve to the brain."}]},{"title":"Musculoskeletal System","questions":[{"section":"The Skeletal System","q":"The skeletal system consists of five components. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Bone","Cartilage","Tendons","Nerves"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The skeletal system consists of bone, cartilage, tendons, joints, and ligaments. Nerves are not part of the skeletal system."},{"section":"The Skeletal System","q":"What are the five functions of bone?","options":["Support, protection, movement, storage, and blood cell formation","Support, protection, digestion, storage, and heat generation","Movement, protection, digestion, blood cell formation, and storage","Support, movement, respiration, storage, and blood cell formation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The five functions of bone are: support, protection, movement, storage (of fat and minerals), and blood cell formation (hematopoiesis)."},{"section":"The Skeletal System","q":"Where does blood cell formation (hematopoiesis) take place?","options":["In the periosteum","In the articular cartilage","In the marrow cavities of certain bones","In the epiphysis only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blood cell formation (hematopoiesis) occurs within the marrow cavities of certain bones."},{"section":"The Skeletal System","q":"The adult human skeleton is made up of how many bones?","options":["186","196","206","216"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The adult skeleton is made up of 206 bones, subdivided into the axial skeleton and the appendicular skeleton."},{"section":"The Skeletal System","q":"Which part of the skeleton includes the spine, skull, and ribs?","options":["Appendicular skeleton","Axial skeleton","Peripheral skeleton","Central skeleton"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The axial skeleton forms the longitudinal axis of the body and includes the spine, skull, and ribs."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Bones are classified into four groups based on their shape. Which of the following lists all four correctly?","options":["Long, short, flat, irregular","Long, short, round, flat","Compact, spongy, flat, irregular","Long, dense, flat, irregular"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The four bone classifications based on shape are: long, short, flat, and irregular bones."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Which is an example of a long bone?","options":["Carpal bone of the wrist","Sternum (breastbone)","Humerus of the arm","Vertebra"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The humerus of the arm is a long bone. Long bones are typically longer than they are wide and are found in the limbs (except the wrist and ankle)."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Which is an example of a short bone?","options":["Femur","Rib","Carpal bone of the wrist","Skull bone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The carpal bones of the wrist are short bones. Short bones are generally cube-shaped and contain mostly spongy bone."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Which is an example of a flat bone?","options":["Vertebra","Femur","Sternum (breastbone)","Carpal bone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sternum (breastbone) is a flat bone. Most bones of the skull, ribs, and sternum are flat bones — thin, flattened and usually curved."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Which is an example of an irregular bone?","options":["Humerus","Skull bone","Rib","Vertebra"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vertebrae are irregular bones. Irregular bones do not fit the other categories and include the bones of the spinal column and the hip bones."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"What is the main function of long bones such as the femur?","options":["Protecting organs","Acting as levers for movement","Providing stability in the wrist","Producing hormones"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Long bones are longer than they are wide and act as levers, allowing movement when muscles pull on them."},{"section":"Classification of Bones","q":"Flat bones such as the skull, ribs and sternum mainly provide:","options":["Stability in the ankle","Leverage for running","Protection of organs and a surface for muscle attachment","Storage of bile"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Flat bones are thin and flat; they protect internal organs (e.g. brain, heart, lungs) and provide broad surfaces for muscle attachment."},{"section":"Bone Anatomy & Cells","q":"What is the name of the fibrous membrane that covers and protects the diaphysis (shaft) of a long bone?","options":["Endosteum","Articular cartilage","Periosteum","Synovial membrane"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The diaphysis is covered and protected by a fibrous connective tissue membrane called the periosteum. Sharpey's fibers secure it to the underlying bone."},{"section":"Bone Anatomy & Cells","q":"Which type of bone cell is responsible for forming new bone?","options":["Osteocytes","Osteoclasts","Osteoblasts","Osteogenic cells"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Osteoblasts are the bone-forming cells. Osteocytes are mature bone cells, osteoclasts break down bone, and osteogenic cells are stem cells."},{"section":"Bone Anatomy & Cells","q":"What is the function of osteoclasts?","options":["To form new bone matrix","To maintain mature bone tissue","To break down bone matrix for remodelling and calcium release","To act as stem cells"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Osteoclasts are bone-destroying cells that break down bone matrix for remodelling and the release of calcium."},{"section":"Joints","q":"What is a joint (also called an articulation)?","options":["A type of bone tissue","The site where two or more bones meet","A fibrous connective tissue that attaches muscle to bone","The outer layer of a bone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Joints (articulations) are the sites where two or more bones meet. They hold the bones together securely and give the rigid skeleton mobility."},{"section":"Joints","q":"Which type of structural joint is freely moveable?","options":["Fibrous joint","Cartilaginous joint","Synovial joint","Suture joint"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Synovial joints are freely moveable. Fibrous joints are generally immovable, and cartilaginous joints are immovable or slightly moveable."},{"section":"Joints","q":"What fluid is found inside a synovial joint cavity?","options":["Aqueous humor","Perilymph","Synovial fluid","Cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The joint cavity of a synovial joint contains lubricating synovial fluid, which reduces friction between the articulating bones."},{"section":"Ligaments & Tendons","q":"What is the difference between a ligament and a tendon?","options":["A ligament attaches muscle to bone; a tendon attaches bone to bone","A ligament attaches bone to bone; a tendon attaches muscle to bone","Both attach muscle to bone, but ligaments are stronger","Both attach bone to bone, but tendons are more flexible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A ligament is a fibrous connective tissue that attaches bone to bone. A tendon is a fibrous connective tissue that attaches muscle to bone."},{"section":"The Muscular System","q":"What are the three types of muscle tissue?","options":["Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth","Skeletal, voluntary, and involuntary","Compact, spongy, and smooth","Striated, cardiac, and flat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The three types of muscle tissue are skeletal, cardiac, and smooth. They differ in cell structure, body location, and how they are stimulated to contract."},{"section":"The Muscular System","q":"The ability of a muscle to shorten and generate force is called:","options":["Extensibility","Elasticity","Excitability","Contractibility"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Contractibility is the ability of muscle tissue to become shorter and thicker (to contract). This sets muscles apart from all other tissue types."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"The sliding filament theory of muscle contraction involves two key proteins. What are they?","options":["Collagen and elastin","Actin and myosin","Calcium and phosphorus","Acetylcholine and ATP"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The sliding filament theory describes how two protein filaments — actin (thin filaments) and myosin (thick filaments) — interact. Myosin heads attach to actin and pull the thin filaments towards the centre of the sarcomere, causing contraction."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"According to the sliding filament theory, what acts as the final trigger for muscle contraction?","options":["Acetylcholine binding directly to myosin","Calcium ions binding to regulatory proteins on the actin filaments","ATP breaking down in the sarcomere","Sodium ions rushing out of the muscle cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Calcium ions are released when an action potential sweeps along the sarcolemma. Calcium binds to the regulatory proteins on the actin filaments, changing their shape and position, which allows myosin to attach and contraction to begin."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"Which protein forms the THICK filaments in a sarcomere?","options":["Actin","Collagen","Troponin","Myosin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Myosin forms the thick filaments. Actin forms the thin filaments."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"From where are calcium ions (Ca²⁺) released to trigger muscle contraction?","options":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The mitochondria","The nucleus","The blood plasma only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ca²⁺ ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (the muscle cell's specialised endoplasmic reticulum) when the muscle is stimulated."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"How do calcium ions allow myosin to bind to actin?","options":["They supply energy to the myosin heads","They move the troponin–tropomyosin complex, uncovering the binding sites on actin","They break down ATP","They join actin filaments together"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At rest, the troponin–tropomyosin complex covers the binding sites on actin. Ca²⁺ binds to troponin, shifting the complex and exposing the binding sites so myosin can attach."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"When myosin heads attach to actin, what do they form?","options":["Sarcomeres","Tendons","Cross-bridges","Synapses"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Myosin heads bind to actin to form cross-bridges, then pull (ratchet) the actin filaments towards the centre of the sarcomere."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"Which molecule provides the energy for the myosin heads to pull on actin?","options":["Glucose directly","Calcium","Acetylcholine","ATP"],"answer":3,"explanation":"ATP provides the energy for the myosin heads to move and to detach from actin so the cycle can repeat."},{"section":"Muscle Contraction","q":"What happens to the sarcomere during contraction?","options":["It shortens as actin filaments are pulled towards its centre","It lengthens","The actin and myosin filaments themselves get shorter","It stays the same length"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The filaments do not shorten — they slide past each other. Actin is pulled towards the centre, so the sarcomere (and the whole muscle) shortens."},{"section":"Coordination Among Muscles","q":"What is the term for the muscle that has the major responsibility for causing a particular movement?","options":["Antagonist","Synergist","Fixator","Prime mover"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The prime mover is the muscle with the major responsibility for causing a particular movement. Muscles that oppose or reverse that movement are called antagonists."}]},{"title":"Nervous System","questions":[{"section":"Organisation","q":"What does the central nervous system (CNS) consist of?","options":["The brain and spinal cord","The cranial and spinal nerves","The sympathetic and parasympathetic systems","All the sense organs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The CNS is made up of the brain and spinal cord, protected by the skull and vertebral column."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"What is the main role of the CNS?","options":["To carry impulses to muscles only","To produce hormones","To transport blood","To process and integrate information"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The CNS is the integration centre: it receives, processes and integrates information and decides on a response."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"What does the peripheral nervous system (PNS) consist of?","options":["The brain only","The spinal cord only","All the nerves outside the CNS — 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves","The brain and the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The PNS includes all nerve tissue outside the CNS: 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves, connecting the CNS to the rest of the body."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"How many pairs of spinal nerves are there?","options":["12","31","33","43"],"answer":1,"explanation":"There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves (and 12 pairs of cranial nerves) in the PNS."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"The somatic nervous system controls:","options":["Involuntary contraction of the heart","Voluntary control of skeletal muscles","Secretion of hormones","Digestion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The somatic nervous system is the division of the PNS that controls voluntary movement of skeletal muscles."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"The autonomic nervous system controls:","options":["Involuntary functions of internal organs, such as heart rate and digestion","Voluntary movements of skeletal muscles","Higher thinking in the cerebral cortex","Sensory input from the skin only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The autonomic nervous system provides involuntary control of internal organs, e.g. heart rate, digestion and pupil size."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"Which effects are produced by the SYMPATHETIC nervous system?","options":["Decreased heart rate and increased digestion","Constricted pupils and slower breathing","Increased heart rate, dilated pupils and inhibited digestion","Increased saliva production and relaxation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sympathetic nervous system produces the 'fight or flight' response: faster heart rate, dilated pupils and inhibited digestion."},{"section":"Organisation","q":"The PARASYMPATHETIC nervous system is responsible for:","options":["The fight or flight response","Voluntary movement","Increasing heart rate","'Rest and digest' — decreasing heart rate and stimulating digestion"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The parasympathetic nervous system produces 'rest and digest' effects: slowing the heart rate and stimulating digestion."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"What is a synapse?","options":["The cell body of a neuron","The junction between two neurons where chemical transmission occurs","A bundle of nerves","The fatty sheath around an axon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A synapse is the junction between two neurons (or a neuron and an effector), where the signal is passed on chemically by neurotransmitters."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"When an action potential arrives at the pre-synaptic terminal, what happens FIRST?","options":["Voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open and Ca²⁺ ions enter the terminal","Neurotransmitters bind to post-synaptic receptors","Acetylcholinesterase breaks down the neurotransmitter","A new action potential is generated"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The arriving action potential opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and Ca²⁺ ions flow into the pre-synaptic terminal. Skipping Ca²⁺ is the most common exam mistake!"},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"What is the role of Ca²⁺ ions in synaptic transmission?","options":["They carry the impulse across the synaptic cleft","They break down neurotransmitters","They trigger synaptic vesicles to fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane","They bind to post-synaptic receptors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ca²⁺ entry triggers the vesicles containing neurotransmitter to fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane, releasing their contents."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"By which process do synaptic vesicles release neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft?","options":["Endocytosis","Osmosis","Active reabsorption","Exocytosis"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Vesicles fuse with the pre-synaptic membrane and release neurotransmitters into the cleft by exocytosis."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"How do neurotransmitters cross the synaptic cleft?","options":["By active transport","By diffusion","Along the axon","In the blood"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Neurotransmitters diffuse across the narrow synaptic cleft to reach receptors on the post-synaptic membrane."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"What happens when neurotransmitters bind to receptors on the post-synaptic membrane?","options":["Ion channels open, producing an EPSP (excitatory) or IPSP (inhibitory)","The pre-synaptic neuron fires again","Ca²⁺ leaves the post-synaptic neuron","The neurotransmitter is converted into hormone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Binding opens ion channels in the post-synaptic membrane, producing an excitatory (EPSP) or inhibitory (IPSP) post-synaptic potential."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"A new action potential is generated in the post-synaptic neuron only if:","options":["Calcium leaves the cell","The neurotransmitter is broken down first","The threshold is reached","An IPSP is produced"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If enough depolarisation occurs for the threshold to be reached, a new action potential is generated in the post-synaptic neuron."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"How are neurotransmitters removed from the synaptic cleft?","options":["They are excreted in urine immediately","They stay in the cleft permanently","They diffuse into the blood and are carried to the brain","They are broken down by enzymes (e.g. acetylcholinesterase) or taken back up (reuptake)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Neurotransmitters are broken down by enzymes such as acetylcholinesterase, or reabsorbed by reuptake, so the signal stops."},{"section":"Synaptic Transmission","q":"Which sequence of synaptic transmission is correct?","options":["Neurotransmitter binds → Ca²⁺ enters → vesicles fuse → action potential arrives","Action potential arrives → Ca²⁺ enters → vesicles fuse (exocytosis) → neurotransmitter diffuses and binds → post-synaptic potential","Vesicles fuse → action potential arrives → neurotransmitter broken down → Ca²⁺ enters","Ca²⁺ enters → neurotransmitter broken down → action potential arrives → vesicles fuse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AP arrives → Ca²⁺ channels open, Ca²⁺ enters → vesicles fuse (exocytosis) → neurotransmitter released and diffuses → binds to receptors → EPSP/IPSP → new AP if threshold reached → neurotransmitter removed."},{"section":"Neurotransmitters","q":"Which neurotransmitter acts at neuromuscular junctions and in the PNS?","options":["Acetylcholine (ACh)","Dopamine","Serotonin","GABA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter at neuromuscular junctions (nerve to skeletal muscle) and is widely used in the PNS."},{"section":"Neurotransmitters","q":"Which is the major INHIBITORY neurotransmitter in the CNS?","options":["Noradrenaline","Acetylcholine","GABA","Dopamine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS, reducing the likelihood of the post-synaptic neuron firing."},{"section":"Neurotransmitters","q":"Which neurotransmitter is involved in mood, sleep and appetite, with low levels linked to depression?","options":["Dopamine","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","Noradrenaline"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serotonin regulates mood, sleep and appetite. Low levels are associated with depression."},{"section":"Nervous System Disorders","q":"Parkinson's disease is associated with:","options":["Excess serotonin","Demyelination of axons","Too much acetylcholine","Loss of dopamine-producing neurons, causing tremors and rigid, slow movement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In Parkinson's disease, dopamine-producing neurons degenerate. The dopamine deficiency causes tremors, rigidity and slow movement."},{"section":"Nervous System Disorders","q":"How does multiple sclerosis (MS) disrupt the function of the nervous system?","options":["Demyelination of axons slows or blocks nerve conduction, causing muscle weakness and loss of coordination","It destroys dopamine-producing neurons","It increases the speed of nerve impulses","It blocks the production of GABA"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In MS, the myelin sheath around axons is damaged (demyelination). Impulses are slowed or blocked, leading to muscle weakness and loss of coordination."}]},{"title":"Reproductive System","questions":[{"section":"Reproductive Organs","q":"What is the primary reproductive organ in males?","options":["Prostate gland","Testes","Seminal vesicles","Penis"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The testes are the primary male reproductive organs."},{"section":"Reproductive Organs","q":"What are the TWO main products of the testes?","options":["Sperm and testosterone","Eggs and oestrogen","Sperm and progesterone","Semen and FSH"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The testes produce sperm (by spermatogenesis) and the hormone testosterone."},{"section":"Reproductive Organs","q":"What do the ovaries produce?","options":["Sperm and testosterone","Insulin and glucagon","Eggs (ova) and the hormones oestrogen and progesterone","Only oestrogen"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The ovaries are the primary female reproductive organs. They produce eggs (by oogenesis) and the hormones oestrogen and progesterone."},{"section":"Reproductive Organs","q":"Which hormone is mainly responsible for the development of male secondary sexual characteristics?","options":["Oestrogen","Progesterone","Insulin","Testosterone"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Testosterone, produced by the testes, is responsible for male secondary sexual characteristics and supports sperm production."},{"section":"Reproductive Organs","q":"Which two pituitary hormones regulate gamete production in the ovaries and testes?","options":["TSH and ADH","Insulin and glucagon","Adrenaline and cortisol","FSH and LH"],"answer":3,"explanation":"FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) and LH (luteinising hormone) from the pituitary gland control the ovaries and testes."},{"section":"Gametogenesis","q":"What is gametogenesis?","options":["The fusion of sperm and egg","The implantation of an embryo","The division of a zygote","The general term for the production of gametes (sperm or eggs)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Gametogenesis is the general term for the production of gametes: spermatogenesis in males and oogenesis in females."},{"section":"Gametogenesis","q":"Where does spermatogenesis take place?","options":["In the ovaries","In the seminiferous tubules of the testes","In the prostate gland","In the uterus"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sperm are produced from spermatogonia by meiosis in the seminiferous tubules of the testes."},{"section":"Gametogenesis","q":"When does oogenesis begin and when is it completed?","options":["Begins at puberty; completed at menopause","Begins at puberty; completed at ovulation","Begins before birth; completed at fertilisation","Begins at fertilisation; completed at birth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Oogenesis begins before birth (in the developing female foetus) and is only completed at fertilisation."},{"section":"Gametogenesis","q":"Which statement correctly compares spermatogenesis and oogenesis?","options":["Both begin before birth","Both produce four functional gametes","Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty, while oogenesis begins before birth","Oogenesis produces more gametes than spermatogenesis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sperm production is continuous from puberty onwards, whereas oogenesis starts before birth and produces far fewer gametes."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"What type of cell division produces gametes?","options":["Meiosis","Mitosis","Binary fission","Budding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Gametes are produced by meiosis, a type of cell division that halves the chromosome number."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"Meiosis produces:","options":["Two identical diploid cells","Two haploid cells","Four haploid cells from one diploid cell","Four diploid cells"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Meiosis produces four haploid (n) cells from one diploid (2n) parent cell."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"What does 'haploid' mean?","options":["Having two sets of chromosomes (2n)","Having no chromosomes","Having extra chromosomes","Having one set of chromosomes (n)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A haploid cell contains one set of chromosomes (n), which is half the number found in body (diploid) cells."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"How many chromosomes are found in a human gamete?","options":["46","23","92","22"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Human body cells contain 46 chromosomes (diploid). Gametes are haploid and contain 23 chromosomes."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"Why is it important that gametes are haploid?","options":["So that fertilisation restores the diploid number (46) in the zygote","So that gametes can divide by mitosis","So that the zygote has 92 chromosomes","So that gametes can move faster"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When two haploid gametes (23 + 23) fuse at fertilisation, the diploid number of 46 chromosomes is restored."},{"section":"Meiosis","q":"The fusion of a sperm and an egg at fertilisation forms:","options":["A haploid zygote","A gamete","A diploid zygote","A polar body"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fertilisation produces a diploid zygote, which then divides by mitosis to form an embryo."},{"section":"Spermatogenesis vs Oogenesis","q":"From each precursor cell, how many functional sperm does spermatogenesis produce?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Spermatogenesis produces 4 functional sperm cells from each precursor cell (spermatocyte)."},{"section":"Spermatogenesis vs Oogenesis","q":"From each precursor cell, what does oogenesis produce?","options":["Four functional eggs","One functional egg (ovum) plus 3 polar bodies","Two functional eggs and two polar bodies","One egg and one sperm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Oogenesis produces only 1 functional egg (ovum) plus 3 non-functional polar bodies. This is the key difference from spermatogenesis."},{"section":"Spermatogenesis vs Oogenesis","q":"What happens to the polar bodies produced in oogenesis?","options":["They are non-functional and are discarded (degenerate)","They develop into sperm","They become additional eggs","They form the placenta"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Polar bodies are small, non-functional cells that are discarded. Unequal division means the egg keeps most of the cytoplasm."},{"section":"Reproductive Disorders","q":"How does polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) affect reproduction?","options":["It blocks sperm delivery","A hormonal imbalance disrupts ovulation, reducing fertility","It causes excess sperm production","It prevents meiosis in the testes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In PCOS, a hormonal imbalance disrupts ovulation, so eggs are released irregularly or not at all, reducing fertility."},{"section":"Reproductive Disorders","q":"Endometriosis can reduce fertility because:","options":["Abnormal growth of tissue similar to the uterine lining causes pain and can impair implantation","It stops the production of testosterone","It increases the number of eggs released","It damages the seminiferous tubules"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In endometriosis, tissue similar to the uterine lining grows abnormally (often outside the uterus), causing pain and potentially impairing implantation and fertility."},{"section":"Reproductive Disorders","q":"How does erectile dysfunction affect reproduction?","options":["It prevents meiosis","It stops egg production","It impairs the delivery of sperm","It changes the number of chromosomes in sperm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Erectile dysfunction impairs the delivery of sperm during intercourse, which can reduce the chance of fertilisation."}]}];
